==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|contrarie}}, compare {{cog|fr|contraire}}, from {{der|en|fro|contraire}}, from {{der|en|la|contrārius||opposite, opposed, contrary}}, from {{m|la|contrā||against}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈkɒntɹəɹi/|/kənˈtɹɛəɹi/|q3=haplology|/ˈkɒntɹi/|a=UK}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈkɑntɹɛɹi/|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|En-us-contrary.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɛəɹi|s=3|q=some pronunciations}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# [[opposite|Opposite]]; in an opposite direction; in opposition; [[adverse]].
#: {{ux|en|'''contrary''' winds}}
#* {{RQ:King James Version|Leviticus|26|21||And if ye walk '''contrary''' unto me, and will not hearken unto me{{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare All's Well|act=III|scene=v|passage=We have lost our labour; they are gone a '''contrary''' way.}}
# Opposed; contradictory; inconsistent.
#* {{RQ:Whewell Inductive Sciences|edition=new|volume=I|chapter=Sequel to [[w:Nicolaus Copernicus|Copernicus]]—The Reception and Development of the Copernican Theory|section=4 (The Copernican System Opposed on Theological Grounds)|page=419|passage=[[w:Galileo Galilei|Galileo [Galilei]]]'s zeal for his opinions soon led him again to bring the question under the notice of the Pope, and the result was a declaration of the Inquisition that the doctrine of the earth's motion appeared to be '''contrary''' to the sacred scripture.}}
# Given to opposition; perverse; wayward.
#: {{ux|en|a '''contrary''' disposition; a '''contrary''' child}}

