===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|obsolete}} To [[oppose]]; to [[frustrate]].
#* {{RQ:Latimer Lords Prayer|part=2|chapter=Edward VI 7|folio=93|passage=You that be of the court, & eſpecially ye ſworn chaplains beware of a leſſon that a great man taught me at my firſt coming to the court he told me for a good will, he thoughte it wel. He ſayd vnto me. You muſt beware how ſo euer ye do that ye '''cõtrary''' not the king, let him haue his ſaiyngs, folow him, go with him. [[marry#Interjection|Mary]] out vpon this counſel, ſhal I ſay, as he ſayes?}}
#* {{RQ:Montaigne Florio Essayes|chapter=47|book=I|passage=The Athenians having left the enemie in their owne land, for to pass into ''Sicilie'', had very ill successe, and were much '''contraried''' by fortune{{nb...}}.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} To [[impugn]].
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} To [[contradict]] (someone or something).
#* {{RQ:Mlry MrtDrthr|12.77|2|X|lxxvii|passage=thus wilfully sir Palomydes dyd bataille with yow<br>& as for hym sir I was not gretely aferd but I dred fore laūcelot that knew yow not<br>Madame said Palomydes ye maye saye what so ye wyll<br>I maye not '''contrary''' yow but by my knyghthode I knewe not sir Tristram}}
<!--Prior quote:#*:‘Madame,’ seyde Sir Palomydes, ‘ye may say what ye woll&mdash;I may nat '''contrary''' you&mdash;but, by my knyghthod, I knew nat my lorde Sir Trystram.’-->
#* {{RQ:Montaigne Florio Essayes|chapter=12|book=II|passage=I finde them everie one in his turne to have reason, although they '''contrary''' one another.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} To do the opposite of (''someone'' or ''something'').
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} To act inconsistently or perversely; to act in opposition ''to''.
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} To argue; to debate; to uphold an opposite opinion.
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} To be self-contradictory; to become reversed.

